{
  "parent_public_research_commit": "6d33465fa698401d44c06c1ec8d1471e02b1924a",
  "parent_tree": "2760eed30b786b4605cdce14f11340974fefaf8d",
  "contributors": "Pang fixed the original row unit and required each part of the error to be paid there. Hearthline selected and bounded the finite smoothing, zero contributions, prime powers and integer transfer. Büthe, Johnston, Platt and Trudgian provide the precisely cited analytical inputs.",
  "paper_scope": "The original two-sided one-thirteenth test, and both guarded primorial comparisons, hold at all cuts of index 22 and through the first half of index 23. These are finite consequences of the published finite zero height 3e12 and unconditional treatment of the remaining zeros. The all-board result remains open.",
  "formal_scope": "Each named finite index and the partial observed horizon are specialized in Lean from the explicit bound on the difference of theta errors between that root and its later cuts. The paper proves the numerical analytic input with the published finite-height theorem. The source finite-zero calculation is not reimplemented.",
  "review": {
    "type": "author self-review with a separate arithmetic evaluation",
    "definitions": "Any source half-weight convention is charged a whole extra log at the observed coordinate or at the root. Repeated prime powers are bounded using theta(v)<=v log(4), the square-root term, and a rounded-up cube-root bound for all k>=3.",
    "high_tail": "Only the first two zero ranges use the finite critical-line result; the tail above c/epsilon is paid by the unrestricted bound in Buthes corrected Proposition 3. The middle condition is a*c/epsilon>=1000.",
    "interval": "For each full slab all increasing positive numerators are evaluated at the upper endpoint, and the log smoothing denominator at a rigorously lower bound at the left endpoint. Endpoint sampling alone is never used as a uniform proof.",
    "arithmetic": "Outward rational enclosures supply each bound. A separate 75-digit decimal evaluation checks the four analytic components on every recorded slab. The positive Bessel series and tail also independently establish the factor used.",
    "next_full_head": "A conservative upper envelope at the full next endpoint exceeds its quota. This diagnoses this choice of estimate and gives no counterexample to the actual inequality."
  },
